
In a biotechnological company they are studying the use of a proteolytic enzyme and an inhibitor
They have isolated and sequenced both:

Enzyme:


Inhibitor:
GMENQEVVLSIDAIQEPEQIKFNMSLKNQSERAIEFQFSTGQKFELVVYDSEHKERYRYSKEKMFTQAFQNLTLESGETYDFSDVWKEVPEPGTYEVKVTFKGRAENLKQVQAVQQFEVK
 

Some questions have arised, and after modelling both they have used docking to check the ability of the inhibitor to hamper the enzymatic function.
In the folder SCRATCH are the last minute results of the ftdock program. The chain of the enzyme was named E and the chain of the inhibitor I, and
the execution of the program was:

ftdock -static enzyme.pdb -mobile inhibitor.pdb

NOTE 1: the models were constructed with the full sequence.
NOTE 2: Use the database of PDB sequences  "pdb_seqres.fa"

Points	Question 
======	========

0.5 	a)   What kind of enzyme and inhibitor are these proteins. What's the PFAM family domain(s) of each?
             Obtain the PFAM domain(s) and name it(them) as: enzyme_1.hmmm, enzyme_2.hmmm etc. and inhibitor_1.hmm inhibitor_2.hmm etc.
             for both enzyme and inhibtor sequences, respectively.
1.0 	b)   Obtain the structure of both molecules with the side-chains and name them: enzyme_0.pdb and inhbitor.pdb
0.5 	c)   If the structure of the enzyme has some problem, locate the problem by identifying the residues in the structure 
             and explain a reason for this problem. Make an image to show the wrong region (p31c.png)
    	d)   Try to modify the structure and improve the quality of the structure. 
             Proof your reasoning:
0.5 	d.1) Name the new structure enzyme.pdb
0.5 	d.2) Proof with a number the quality of your model and show with an image if the structure is 
             better than the original (p31d2.png)

1.0     e)   Obtain between 1 and 10 poses (conformations) of the interaction between enzyme and inhibitor ensuring that at least the 
             active site of the enzyme interacts with the inibitor (Name the PDBs as Complex_1g.pdb, Complex_2g.pdb etc.) 
1.0     f)   Select one conformation with the best score by FTDOCK that you also consider is the most representative of these solutions 
             (i.e. this is the most often repeated or similar pose among all solutions). Identify a close homolog of the enzyme inhibited by
             eglin-C (an inhibitor from Leech) and compare it with your selected complex using a superimposition. Safe the PDB of the 
             superimposition as  p31f.pdb and use an image to identify the region that you think is responsible for the inhibition in both
             inhibitors (use a figure as p31f_fig.png)
1.0     g)   Compare the sequences of the peptide regions responsible for the inhibition of both inhibitors (for eaxample with an alignment)
             and give your arguments on the potential of a peptide to be used as an inhibitor. 
              


